Organizational Unit (OU)

An Organizational Unit (OU) is a container in Active Directory used to organize:

Users
Computers
Groups

Example:

Company
│

├── HR
├── Finance
├── IT
└── Sales

Each department can contain its own users, computers, and groups.

Why Companies Use OUs

Benefits:

Organize users by department
Easier administration
Easier troubleshooting
Easier application of security policies
Easier Group Policy management
OU vs Group
Organizational Unit (OU)

Purpose:

Organizes Active Directory objects.
Helps administrators manage departments.
Group

Purpose:

Collection of users.
Used to assign permissions to resources.


Organizational Unit (OU)

An Organizational Unit (OU) is a container in Active Directory used to organize objects such as:

Users
Computers
Groups

OUs can also be used as a scope for applying Group Policies.

Simple memory:
OU = Organization
2. Security Group

A Security Group is a collection of users or computers used to assign permissions and access to resources.

Instead of assigning permissions individually to every user, administrators can assign permissions to a security group and then add users to that group.

Example:
Finance Security Group
        ↓
Finance Shared Folder
        ↓
Users receive access
Benefits
Easier permission management
Saves administrative time
Reduces configuration mistakes
Simplifies access control
Memory:
Security Group = Access

⚙️ Group Policy Object (GPO)

A GPO (Group Policy Object) is a collection of configuration and security settings that can be centrally applied to users and computers.

Examples:

Password policies
Security settings
Desktop configuration
System restrictions
Software configuration
Important distinction:
OU
↓
Organizes objects

Security Group
↓
Provides access

GPO
↓
Applies configuration
Memory:
OU = WHERE
Security Group = WHO gets ACCESS
GPO = WHAT SETTINGS
